Goal:
. To retrieve the password for the next level by escaping the
  uppercase-only shell.

Connect:
. ssh [bandit32@bandit.labs.overthewire.org](mailto:bandit32@bandit.labs.overthewire.org) -p 2220

Approach:
. After logging in, I noticed that the shell converted every command I
  entered into uppercase, which prevented normal Linux commands from working.
. I realized that the shell was restricting the commands by converting
  lowercase input to uppercase.
. I used '$0' to start a new shell, which allowed me to escape the
  restricted shell and execute normal commands.
. I then used 'cat' to read the password file for the next level.

Command:
. $0
. cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit33

Breakdown:
. '$0' refers to the name of the currently running shell and can be used
  to start another shell from within the restricted environment.
. Once the new shell was started, the uppercase restriction was bypassed.
. 'cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit33' displays the password for Bandit 33.

Result:
. I successfully escaped the uppercase-only shell and retrieved the
password for Bandit 33.

Key takeaway:
. Restricted shells may limit normal command execution, but understanding
  how the shell works can reveal ways to escape those restrictions.
. '$0' can be used to invoke the current shell and, in this level, escape
  the uppercase-only restriction.
